  "initial_reflection": {
    "summary": "The run developed strong offensive scaling but traded away too much survivability, leaving the Ironclad at 39/80 HP for the final Sentry fight and dead at 2 HP. The most reusable issues were repeatedly favoring damage over defensive card rewards, accepting an avoidable curse, and upgrading at a later campfire instead of preserving HP.",
    "lessons": [
      {
        "category": "CARD_REWARD",
        "title": "Stop adding attacks once damage is already covered",
        "situation": "When the deck already has several strong attacks and scaling cards but still mostly relies on starter Defends for mitigation.",
        "lesson": "Prefer defensive or hybrid rewards, or skip the reward, rather than continuing to add attacks. In particular, reassess choices like Carnage over Ghostly Armor and Whirlwind over Iron Wave when the deck lacks non-starter block.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "The run chose Carnage over Ghostly Armor at Floor 3 and later chose Whirlwind over Iron Wave at Floor 11.",
          "The final 19-card deck contains four Defends but no Ghostly Armor or Iron Wave, alongside Carnage+, Uppercut+, Thunderclap, Rampage, and Whirlwind."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The final deck had substantial damage and scaling but no added defensive card, making HP loss harder to prevent in multi-enemy fights. The repeated attack selections were therefore more consequential than any single card choice.",
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "category": "EVENT",
        "title": "Do not take curse-for-gold events without a removal plan",
        "situation": "When an event offers gold in exchange for a curse and there is no confirmed near-term way to remove the curse.",
        "lesson": "Decline the curse trade unless the gold is urgently needed and removal is realistically available soon; otherwise the permanent deck dilution is not worth the short-term cash.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "At Floor 7, the run chose to gain 175 gold and become cursed with Doubt.",
          "Doubt remains in the final deck, and the earlier shop's card-removal option was not purchased."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The gold was obtained, but the curse stayed permanently in the deck through the end of the run. This is especially costly in a small Act 1 deck where every draw matters.",
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "category": "REST",
        "title": "Preserve HP at the later campfire when damage is already sufficient",
        "situation": "At a later Act 1 campfire, when entering at moderate or low HP, lacking potions, and already possessing enough damage to handle ordinary encounters.",
        "lesson": "Favor resting over another marginal upgrade when the deck is already offense-heavy. The extra HP can be more valuable than upgrading another attack before the boss path.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "At Floor 10 the run had 55/80 HP and chose to smith Carnage rather than rest.",
          "The final Sentry combat began at 39/80 HP, and the final build had no potions."
        ],
        "reasoning": "Choosing the upgrade left the run with limited HP margin for the remaining encounters, while the deck already contained multiple offensive tools. The trajectory does not prove a rest would have guaranteed victory, but it supports treating HP preservation as the safer priority in this situation.",
        "confidence": "medium"
      }
    ]
  },
